const b=r.locals.find(x=>x.symbol==="beta0");
b.declaration="Set beta0=2*sigma-3/2 when 0<sigma<1/2, beta0=-1/2-epsilon when sigma=1/2, and beta0=sigma-1 when 1/2<sigma<=1, using the fixed common source-admissible loss epsilon.";
b.objects=["sigma","epsilon"];
r.locals.push({symbol:"epsilon",type:"real loss",scope:"R002/argument",start:"S002",end:"S003",kind:"witness",declaration:"Choose once and for all a common midpoint loss epsilon with 0<epsilon<=1/8 admissible in both E003 and E004. Each input holds for every sufficiently small positive loss at sigma=1/2; half the minimum of their two positive thresholds and 1/8 supplies such a witness. This choice is independent of sigma, tau, a, w, M, all product inputs and Fourier support.",from_inputs:["E003","E004"],from_local:[],objects:[]});
r.locals.push({symbol:"s",type:"real stage-time offset",scope:"R002/argument",start:"S003",end:"S003",kind:"arbitrary",declaration:"Fix any s with 0<=s<=tau for the pointwise stage estimate, and then quantify s over this interval.",from_inputs:[],from_local:[],objects:["tau"]});
r.argument[1].text="First choose a common midpoint source loss epsilon with 0<epsilon<=1/8. This is justified by the exact 'any fixed sufficiently small positive' quantifiers in E003 and E004 at sigma=1/2: each provides a positive threshold below which every positive loss is allowed, so half the minimum of the two thresholds and 1/8 is an admissible common choice. Fix this choice once, independently of all inputs, times and support. We do not assert that 1/8 itself is source-admissible. Now fix sigma,tau,a,w,M and assume L1: 0<sigma<=1, 0<tau<=1, a>=0, w is constant mean-zero H^sigma, and ||w||_(H^sigma)<=M. Set a_sigma=1/2-sigma,1/8,0 in the three respective ranges sigma<1/2, sigma=1/2, sigma>1/2. These choices satisfy 0<=a_sigma<1. For sigma!=1/2, E004 applies at its stated exponent a(sigma)=a_sigma. At sigma=1/2, apply E004 with the admissible epsilon to any f0 in H^(1/2) and g0 in H^(1/8): g0 belongs to H^epsilon and ||g0||_(H^epsilon)<=||g0||_(H^(1/8)), because <k> >=1 and epsilon<=1/8 give <k>^(2*epsilon)<=<k>^(1/4) term by term in the defining nonnegative Fourier square sums. Thus E004 gives the required product bound with H^(1/8) on the second input, without specializing its loss to 1/8. The same weighted-sum argument proves H^v embeds contractively into H^u whenever u<=v. Conversion from unnormalized norms introduces only the fixed bilinear factor sqrt(2*pi); the fixed epsilon dependence is absorbed into C_sigma. Put beta0=2*sigma-3/2, -1/2-epsilon, sigma-1 in the three respective ranges. Then a_sigma and 1+sigma lie in [beta0,1+sigma], with positive denominator 1+sigma-beta0. At beta=a_sigma the E003 time exponents are (1/2+2*sigma)/(5/2-sigma), 11/(16+8*epsilon), and (1+sigma)/2, respectively. In the first case the exponent minus sigma has positive numerator sigma^2-sigma/2+1/2=(sigma-1/4)^2+7/16. In the midpoint case 0<epsilon<=1/8 gives 11/(16+8*epsilon)>=11/17>1/2=sigma. In the last case (1+sigma)/2>=sigma because sigma<=1. At beta=1+sigma the exponent is zero for this admissible beta0.";
r.argument[1].objects.push("epsilon");
r.argument[1].produces[0].statement="Under L1, 0<=a_sigma<1, and for every f0 in H^sigma and g0 in H^a_sigma, ||f0*g0||_2<=C_sigma||f0||_(H^sigma)||g0||_(H^a_sigma). Moreover a_sigma and 1+sigma lie in [beta0,1+sigma], 1+sigma-beta0>0, (1+sigma-a_sigma)/(1+sigma-beta0)>=sigma, and (1+sigma-(1+sigma))/(1+sigma-beta0)=0. The loss epsilon is fixed, satisfies 0<epsilon<=1/8, and is admissible for both midpoint source assertions; constants may absorb dependence on this fixed choice.";
r.argument[1].produces[0].objects.push("epsilon");
r.argument[2].text="Under L1, the multipliers in D004 give S_u S_v=S_(u+v), preserve each H^r norm by modulus one, and preserve reality and zero mean. For smooth inputs direct substitution in B and then Stage gives F_a(a+s;w,w)=S_(-a)F_0(s;S_a w,S_a w) for every 0<=s<=tau. Indeed B_(a+r)(w,w)=S_(-a)B_r(S_a w,S_a w), and integrate r from 0 to s. To justify the same identity and the ordinary suprema for rough H^sigma inputs, first use E003 at grid base t_0=0 and beta=1+sigma. For every constant mean-zero pair it is a uniform bilinear bound into H^(1+sigma) over s in [0,tau], since its exponent is zero, with the admissible loss epsilon at the midpoint. Mean-zero Fourier truncations converge in H^sigma, as their squared errors are tails of the defining summable weighted square series. Bilinearity and this uniform bound show that their stage paths are uniformly Cauchy in H^(1+sigma): the difference of two diagonal stages is the sum of the bilinear stages on (difference,first input) and (second input,difference). Completeness therefore gives a uniform limit. Each truncated path is continuous in s, so the limit is continuous; the smooth conjugation identity and the isometry S_(-a) give the corresponding continuous extension at base a and the same identity there. This is the Stage extension of its defining smooth integral. Now apply E003 at base zero, time s in [0,tau], and inputs S_a w to beta=a_sigma, using the admissible beta0 and interval and exponent checks in F002. The normalized lower-order bound is C_sigma*tau^theta*||w||_(H^sigma)^2, where theta=(1+sigma-a_sigma)/(1+sigma-beta0)>=sigma. It holds on truncations and passes to the limit: H^(1+sigma) convergence implies H^a_sigma convergence by the weighted-sum norm comparison proved in S002. For 0<tau<=1, tau^theta<=tau^sigma. The upper-endpoint application already gives C_sigma*||w||_(H^sigma)^2. The fixed normalization factors and fixed loss dependence are absorbed into C_sigma. Since ||w||_(H^sigma)<=M, the two pointwise estimates are bounded by C_sigma*M^2*tau^sigma and C_sigma*M^2. Quantify s and take the suprema of these continuous paths to obtain both asserted stage estimates at every real base a>=0, with C_(M,sigma) and no endpoint or support dependence. Discharging L1 and universally quantifying its data proves the stage clause. The product bound and 0<=a_sigma<1 from F002 are independent of tau,a,w,M; for each 0<sigma<=1 instantiate those auxiliary data with tau=1,a=0,w=0,M=0, which satisfy L1, to export the product clause for arbitrary product inputs with no auxiliary hypotheses. Together with the unconditional F001 this proves the full conjunction N002. The private choice epsilon disappears from the exported assertion; only its fixed contribution to constants is retained.";
r.argument[2].objects.push("epsilon","s");
r.applications[1].bindings="Use the current sigma in (0,1]. For sigma!=1/2 use a(sigma)=a_sigma. At sigma=1/2 specialize the source loss to the fixed common admissible epsilon in (0,1/8], giving a(sigma)=epsilon, not 1/8; apply to arbitrary f0 in H^(1/2) and g0 in H^(1/8), regarded as H^epsilon by the displayed norm comparison.";
r.applications[1].hypothesis_checks=["L1 supplies 0<sigma<=1. The 'any fixed sufficiently small' quantifiers of E004 and E003 at sigma=1/2 provide positive thresholds, hence the chosen common epsilon below both thresholds and <=1/8 is genuinely admissible. The nonmidpoint exponent choices are exactly those of E004.","Outside the midpoint the product pair has exactly the source regularities. At the midpoint the first input is H^(1/2), and the second is H^epsilon with norm at most its H^(1/8) norm by termwise comparison of the weighted Fourier square sums."];
r.applications[2].bindings="Use the sufficiently-small midpoint quantifier of E003 at sigma=1/2, together with E004's, to choose the fixed common admissible epsilon in (0,1/8]. For the current sigma inspect beta=a_sigma and beta=1+sigma with beta0=2*sigma-3/2 below the midpoint, -1/2-epsilon at the midpoint, and sigma-1 above. The actual stage applications are at base zero in S003.";
r.applications[2].hypothesis_checks=["L1 supplies 0<sigma<=1 and tau>0. The planned source inputs S_a w are constant mean-zero H^sigma and are norm-preserving transforms, and the stage is at base zero and time in [0,tau]. The exact multiplier and conjugation identities are established in S003 before the stage applications.","The common epsilon is chosen below the positive sufficiently-small thresholds of both midpoint assertions, rather than set equal to 1/8. The displayed case calculation proves that both beta choices lie in [beta0,1+sigma], with positive denominator, and computes their exponents for this admissible beta0."];
r.applications[3].bindings="At grid base t_0=0, time s in [0,tau], and constant inputs f=g=S_a w, use beta=a_sigma and the source beta0 from F002, with the fixed admissible epsilon at sigma=1/2. Apply also on Fourier truncations to pass the bound to the continuous extension. Transfer to base a by the proved Stage conjugation identity.";
r.applications[3].hypothesis_checks=["L1 gives 0<sigma<=1, tau>0 and constant mean-zero H^sigma w; multipliers and truncations preserve zero mean and the required regularity, with the multiplier preserving the norm and truncations decreasing it. Base zero and 0<=s<=tau meet the grid-step condition.","F002 uses an actually admissible midpoint loss and verifies beta=a_sigma lies in the source interval with theta>=sigma. The upper-endpoint construction gives H^(1+sigma) convergence of stage paths, which implies H^a_sigma convergence by the weighted-sum comparison, so the lower-order bounds pass to the extension."];
r.applications[4].bindings="At grid base t_0=0 and time s in [0,tau], use beta=1+sigma and beta0 as in F002. Use the general constant mean-zero H^sigma input pair in E003 for S_a w, its Fourier truncations, and their bilinear differences to construct the continuous extension; the exponent is zero. Conjugate by S_(-a) to obtain the estimate at arbitrary base a.";
r.applications[4].hypothesis_checks=["L1 gives the sigma and step conditions. S_a w and its Fourier truncations and their differences are constant mean-zero H^sigma inputs, and base zero with s in [0,tau] is a permitted source grid step.","F002 proves beta=1+sigma is in the allowed interval with positive denominator, using the common genuinely admissible epsilon at the midpoint. The numerator is zero independently of its numerical value, giving the uniform bound used for the extension and the upper-stage estimate."];
store("repair",{inference:r});text(await tools.check_draft({artifact:{inference:r}}));
